    "## 5. Create Rolling Statistical Features\n",
    "\n",
    "### Concept: Rolling Window Features\n",
    "\n",
    "Rolling features look at a **window of recent readings** and compute statistics. This tells the model about recent sensor behavior.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Rolling mean** = average of last N readings\n",
    "  - If current reading is far from rolling mean, it might be anomalous\n",
    "- **Rolling standard deviation** = how variable the last N readings are\n",
    "  - High std = sensor is volatile/unstable\n",
    "  - Low std = sensor is stable\n",
    "\n",
    "### Window Sizes:\n",
    "- **5 observations** = short-term trend (about 10 seconds if regular 2s sampling)\n",
    "- **15 observations** = medium-term trend (about 30 seconds if regular 2s sampling)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Important: We do NOT describe windows as exact seconds\n",
    "Because our data has repeated timestamps and gaps, \"5 observations\" is not exactly 10 seconds. We describe the window size in **observations**, not time.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Pandas Function: `.rolling(window=N).mean()` / `.std()`\n",
    "- `df['col'].rolling(window=5).mean()` computes mean of current + previous 4 rows\n",
    "- First N-1 rows have NaN (not enough data for full window)\n",
    "- By default, `min_periods=None` means all N values must be present; we can use `min_periods=1` to start calculating with fewer values, but we'll keep default to be conservative\n",
    "\n",
    "### Gap Handling\n",
    "Later we will handle timestamp gaps (>10 seconds) by resetting rolling windows. For now, we compute rolling features on the full sorted dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
